## Chain of Responsibility (CoR)

| **Purpose** | Decouple a *sender* of a request from its *receivers* by giving multiple objects a chance to handle the request. The request travels along a chain until one of the handlers claims it (or until the chain ends). |
|------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **When to use** | • You have many potential handlers and you don’t want the sender to know which one will process the request.<br>• Handlers can be added or removed at runtime.<br>• You want to keep the system extensible (open‑for‑extension, closed‑for‑modification). |
| **Benefits** | • Low coupling between request senders and handlers.<br>• Easy to add new handlers (just add a new class and plug it into the chain).<br>• Handlers can decide to stop propagation or pass it along. |
| **Drawbacks** | • The request can be processed by multiple handlers, which may be undesirable if you need a single handler.<br>• Harder to debug: it’s not obvious who will handle a request.<br>• The order of handlers matters; changing it can affect behavior. |

---

## Classic Structural Diagram

```
        +-------------+          +-------------+          +-------------+
        |  Client     |  -> req  |   Handler   |  -> next  |   Handler   |
        +-------------+         +-------------+          +-------------+
                                            |  ...
                                            |
                                            v
                                      +-------------+
                                      |  Concrete   |
                                      |   Handler   |
                                      +-------------+
```

- **Handler** – abstract base (or interface) that defines the `Handle` method and a reference to the *next* handler.
- **ConcreteHandler** – implements `Handle`; decides whether it can process the request, processes it, then optionally forwards it.

---

## C# Implementation – A Logging Example

We’ll build a tiny logging system that supports three levels: **Info**, **Warning**, **Error**.  
Each logger will only write messages that match its level, but will always pass the request further so that higher‑level logs are also emitted if desired.

```csharp
using System;

namespace ChainOfResponsibilityDemo
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Log levels – simplest enumeration for demo purposes.
    /// </summary>
    public enum LogLevel
    {
        Info,
        Warning,
        Error
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The abstract handler defines the interface for handling a request
    /// and storing a reference to the next handler.
    /// </summary>
    public abstract class Logger
    {
        protected Logger _next;

        /// <summary>
        /// Link the next handler in the chain.
        /// </summary>
        public void SetNext(Logger next)
        {
            _next = next;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// The entry point for requests.
        /// </summary>
        public void Log(string message, LogLevel level)
        {
            if (CanHandle(level))
            {
                Write(message);
            }

            // Forward to the next handler if it exists.
            _next?.Log(message, level);
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Determines if this handler is responsible for the given level.
        /// </summary>
        protected abstract bool CanHandle(LogLevel level);

        /// <summary>
        /// Actually write the message somewhere.
        /// </summary>
        protected abstract void Write(string message);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Concrete logger that writes Info messages to the console.
    /// </summary>
    public class InfoLogger : Logger
    {
        protected override bool CanHandle(LogLevel level) => level == LogLevel.Info;

        protected override void Write(string message)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[INFO] {message}");
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Concrete logger that writes Warning messages to the console.
    /// </summary>
    public class WarningLogger : Logger
    {
        protected override bool CanHandle(LogLevel level) => level == LogLevel.Warning;

        protected override void Write(string message)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[WARNING] {message}");
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Concrete logger that writes Error messages to the console.
    /// </summary>
    public class ErrorLogger : Logger
    {
        protected override bool CanHandle(LogLevel level) => level == LogLevel.Error;

        protected override void Write(string message)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[ERROR] {message}");
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Demo client that builds the chain and sends requests.
    /// </summary>
    public static class Program
    {
        public static void Main()
        {
            // Create handlers
            var errorLogger   = new ErrorLogger();
            var warningLogger = new WarningLogger();
            var infoLogger    = new InfoLogger();

            // Assemble chain: Info → Warning → Error
            infoLogger.SetNext(warningLogger);
            warningLogger.SetNext(errorLogger);

            // Send a few log messages
            infoLogger.Log("This is an informational message.", LogLevel.Info);
            infoLogger.Log("This is a warning. Check your input.", LogLevel.Warning);
            infoLogger.Log("Something went wrong! Immediate action needed.", LogLevel.Error);

            // Output:
            // [INFO] This is an informational message.
            // [WARNING] This is a warning. Check your input.
            // [ERROR] Something went wrong! Immediate action needed.
        }
    }
}
```

### How It Works

1. **Client** builds the chain.  
   `infoLogger` is the first handler; it knows the next handler (`warningLogger`), which in turn knows the next (`errorLogger`).

2. **Request** (`Log`) is sent to the first handler (`infoLogger`).  
   - `infoLogger` checks `CanHandle(LogLevel.Info)` → true → writes the message.  
   - It then forwards the same request to the next handler (`warningLogger`).  
   - `warningLogger` sees that it cannot handle `Info` → passes it on.  
   - `errorLogger` sees it cannot handle `Info` either → chain ends.

3. A **Warning** request behaves similarly: `infoLogger` passes it to `warningLogger`, which writes it and forwards it to `errorLogger`.  
   Since `errorLogger` cannot handle a warning, the chain ends.

4. An **Error** request is only processed by `errorLogger`, which writes it; lower‑level loggers simply forward it and do nothing.

